<!--
  Athena's navigate-to-pose tree.

  This is Nav2's stock navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml with
  two recovery behaviours removed, Spin and BackUp, and the remaining
  recoveries reordered so the cheapest one runs first and the obstacle
  memory is thrown away last.

  Why no Spin or BackUp. The stock recoveries assume a robot that can see
  where it is about to move. Athena cannot. The D435i covers 87 degrees ahead
  and there is no rear-facing sensor at all, so:

    Spin 1.57 rad   rotates the rover through 273 degrees it cannot see,
                    using costmap cells that were last observed some time
                    ago and, with VIO at 2 to 4 Hz indoors, were written
                    with a heading that was already stale.
    BackUp 0.30 m   reverses a 1.3 m rover into space it has NEVER observed.
                    There is no sensor pointing that way. It is not a
                    cautious move, it is a blind one.

  Why the global costmap is no longer wiped on the first planning failure
  (2026-10-02). The stock tree clears the whole global costmap whenever one
  ComputePathToPose fails. The global costmap is the only memory this rover
  has of what is beside and behind it, and a single front camera cannot
  rebuild that memory without turning. In the logs every planning failure was
  one of three things, and wiping helped none of them:
    - the goal's TF stamp had expired (clicked goals in odom or base_link),
      which no costmap change can fix;
    - the planner did not acknowledge the goal within default_server_timeout
      because the Jetson was overloaded, which waiting fixes;
    - the goal or the route really was blocked, where wiping makes the planner
      route straight through the obstacle it just forgot (unknown space is
      plannable), the rover drives back into it, and the loop repeats.
  So a failed plan now waits 1 s and tries again.

  Recovery order, once the main branch has failed (each round re-tries the
  main branch first, up to 6 rounds, then the goal fails and control goes
  back to the operator):
    1. Wait 5 s           free: people move, a CPU spike passes, the camera
                          and VIO catch up.
    2. Clear local        removes stale cells around the rover, the usual
                          cause of "collision ahead" that is not real, while
                          the global costmap keeps the side and rear memory
                          the planner routes around.
    3. Clear both         last resort for residue blocking the route.
  FollowPath's own context recovery (clear the local costmap once) is
  unchanged: in the 2026-10-02 logs it is what let the rover drive on after
  each "collision ahead".

  Node names are what nav_status reads from /behavior_tree_log; keep
  "RecoveryActions" and the Recovery* names if you edit this.

  The Spin and BackUp plugins are still loaded by behavior_server and still
  listed in plugin_lib_names, so switching back to the stock tree is just a
  matter of clearing default_nav_to_pose_bt_xml in nav2_params.yaml.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <Wait name="PlanRetryWait" wait_duration="1"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Wait name="RecoveryWait" wait_duration="5"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="RecoveryClearLocal" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
          <Sequence name="RecoveryClearAll">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
